.env
data/

# Key material for the connector — real secp256k1/ed25519 secrets, generated by
# README.md's own steps and mounted into the container. Wildcarded rather than
# listed by name so a re-derived or additional key cannot reintroduce the gap by
# being named slightly differently.
*.key
*.secret
*.pem

# The operator surface's credentials (connector.toml's [operator]), and THE
# ONE PLACE IN THE FLEET WHERE ALL THREE LIVE SIDE BY SIDE. They are three
# different kinds of thing and the names say so:
#
#   operator-bearer.token   a secret outright. Gates the connector's READS,
#                           and the slot app holds a copy to reconcile with.
#   operator-write.keys     the ALLOWLIST — public halves, one keyid per line,
#     (plural)              hand-edited on the box to grant or revoke
#                           authority (ADR 0008). Not secret, but per box, so
#                           a committed copy would put revocation behind a
#                           pull request and create a second source of truth.
#   operator-signing.key    the slot app's PRIVATE ed25519 seed — the
#     (singular)            credential that mutates this hub's routing table.
#
# The third is named `signing` and not `write` on purpose. Called
# `operator-write.key` it would sit one character from `operator-write.keys` in
# this same directory: two files a tab-completion apart, one of them the secret
# that mutates the routing table and the other the file you edit by hand to
# take that authority away. It is caught by `*.key` above and listed here BY
# NAME as well, because a wildcard is a rule you have to know and a name is a
# rule you can read.
operator-bearer.token
operator-write.keys
operator-signing.key
